<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net8.0</TargetFramework>
    <RootNamespace>Content</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>Content</AssemblyName>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <Nullable>disable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>disable</ImplicitUsings>
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>false</GenerateDocumentationFile>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="../core/core.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    THE WORLD, READ OFF A DISK. core/ is the rules; this is everything the rules are pointed at.

    It must NEVER reference Godot, for the same reason core/ must not: a campaign folder is a
    folder, and the thing that reads one should be testable headless, runnable from the sim, and
    still here if the engine ever changes. It uses System.IO deliberately - a campaign lives
    OUTSIDE the .pck (Steam drops subscribed items in a directory of its own), so it is an
    ordinary file on an ordinary disk and Godot's FileAccess would be the wrong tool.

    net8.0 because game/ references it and Godot 4.7 targets net8.0.

    WHY core/ IS NOT SPLIT TO MAKE THIS POSSIBLE. CONVENTIONS.md's split trigger for pulling
    `localization/` out is "content/ needs Localization without needing Combat". That turned out
    not to be the shape of it: reading a statblock means building an `Actor`, which is
    `Characters`, which sits above `Localization` and below `Combat` - so splitting would move the
    line and save nothing. One project with clean namespaces is still the right answer, and the
    trigger can be re-read when something appears that genuinely needs half of core.
  -->

</Project>
